Laryngeal Carcinoma
Respiratory System
Hoarseness >3 weeks, Referred otalgia, Vocal cord mass

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-026
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of dysphagia and new-onset hemoptysis in a patient with chronic hoarseness, requiring urgent assessment to rule out airway compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient with a weeks-long history of voice changes who is now experiencing sudden difficulty swallowing saliva, ear pain, and coughing up blood. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for impending upper airway obstruction and secure a safe swallowing plan prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My voice has been a raspy whisper for a month, but tonight I keep coughing up streaks of blood and swallowing feels like broken glass in my throat.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began gradually with persistent, progressive hoarseness lasting uninterrupted for more than three weeks. Over the past 48 hours, the patient developed severe dysphagia and odynophagia, accompanied by a constant sensation of a physical lump in the throat. Tonight, they developed intermittent hemoptysis and complain of referred otalgia on the right side. The spouse notes unintentional, progressive weight loss over the past two months and prominent halitosis.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but currently refusing oral intake due to severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Heavy smoking history; high risk for dehydration and aspiration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 18 breaths/min | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Anxious, visibly wincing when attempting to swallow, speaking in a strained, breathy whisper.
Physical Exam: Neck examination reveals a firm, fixed, painless cervical lymphadenopathy on the right side. The patient exhibits hoarseness lasting >3 weeks and complains of referred right otalgia. While a vocal cord mass cannot be directly visualized without laryngoscopy, the clinical presentation is highly suspicious for it. Auscultation of the chest and neck reveals no overt inspiratory stridor at rest, indicating the airway is currently patent.
Environmental Observations: Multiple ashtrays present; patient is keeping a spittoon nearby to avoid swallowing saliva.
Medication Reconciliation: Using over-the-counter throat lozenges with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Laryngeal Carcinoma - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Laryngitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The duration of hoarseness (>3 weeks), presence of hemoptysis, and fixed cervical lymphadenopathy.

Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of unilateral referred ear pain, true odynophagia, and palpable neck mass.

Vocal Cord Nodules - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Hemoptysis, significant weight loss, and severe pain, which are atypical for benign nodules.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute escalation of pain and hemoptysis on a background of chronic hoarseness, dysphagia, and fixed cervical lymphadenopathy, painting a highly concerning picture for laryngeal carcinoma. The sudden inability to swallow saliva and new hemoptysis represent a destabilization of the structural lesion. Delaying clinical assessment until the next in-hours period would pose an unacceptable risk of acute airway obstruction (from localized edema or hemorrhage) and severe dehydration. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to confirm airway patency, provide immediate potent analgesia to facilitate swallowing, and mandate urgent specialist referral.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a viscous lidocaine oral rinse before meals and regular paracetamol/ibuprofen for baseline analgesia. Provided an urgent referral for an ENT specialist for direct laryngoscopy and biopsy.
Positive Behavioral Change: Drink a high-calorie liquid supplement (e.g., Ensure) through a wide straw immediately after using the numbing throat rinse; pairing the painless window with liquid nutrition guarantees caloric intake, directly combating your ongoing weight loss and maintaining your physical strength for upcoming treatments.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent ENT review is mandatory this week; patient to contact clinic first thing in the morning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer (call 000) to the Emergency Department if breathing becomes noisy (stridor), if the patient feels they cannot pull air in, or if hemoptysis exceeds a tablespoon of fresh blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This clinical assessment strongly suspects a tumor; formal diagnosis requires a camera examination of the throat and a tissue biopsy by a specialist.
Clinical Course: The throat pain can be temporarily managed, but definitive treatment requires specialist oncology and surgical intervention.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if your breathing becomes noisy or difficult, or if you begin to feel a choking sensation.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The numbing rinse temporarily blocks the pain signals in your throat, allowing you a short window to swallow essential fluids without severe agony.
Actionable Steps: Completely cease all smoking immediately; continued smoke exposure directly inflames the already damaged tissues in your throat, causing the pain and swelling to worsen rapidly.

